# sockstat — real-world examples

`sockstat` is native to BSD. On openSUSE Tumbleweed, use `ss` (from iproute2)
for the same job.

## List listening sockets (BSD)

```sh
sudo sockstat -l
```

## Linux equivalent — listening TCP/UDP sockets with process names

```sh
sudo ss -tulpn
```

## See who is listening on a specific port

```sh
sudo ss -tulpn 'sport = :32400'
```

## Show all established connections

```sh
sudo ss -tanp state established
```

## ss flag reference

| Flag | What it does                     |
| ---- | -------------------------------- |
| -t   | TCP sockets                      |
| -u   | UDP sockets                      |
| -l   | listening sockets only           |
| -p   | show owning process              |
| -n   | numeric ports (no name lookup)   |
